wibo heap model: WIBO_HEAP_MODEL=nt serves HeapCreate'd heaps with a model of the Windows NT heap
(infernal-machine-decomp, lane tool-iclheap, 2026-10-03; applies to decompals/wibo 1.1.0, commit
b80feaae19c1b0b0759d9abf2b6afabc48957c3d).

Why: Intel C/C++ 4.0 (mcpcom.exe 99100, https://github.com/andrewspode/intel-cc-4.0-setup) sorts the
operands of commutative expressions by the address of their symbol records, which it allocates in 64 KiB
pool chunks from its static VC5 CRT's HeapAlloc. Under wibo's mimalloc arena (0x6c000000) every record
address exceeds 2^30 and the generated code differs from what the compiler produces on Windows; with this
mode wibo reproduces the object a native Windows 11 compile gives (and what a model of the NT 4.0 heap
gives). Details: docs/crt.md "A principled 1999 heap model" in
https://github.com/andrewspode/infernal-machine-decomp (or this repository's docs/crt.md).

What it does: with WIBO_HEAP_MODEL=nt in the environment, every heap created by HeapCreate gets an NT
backend allocator (src/ntheap.cpp): 8-byte HEAP_ENTRY headers and granularity, FreeLists[1..127] (oldest
first) and a size-ordered FreeLists[0], split at the front, coalescing, page-wise commit of 1 MiB segments
reserved bottom-up through wibo's VirtualAlloc, decommit of large free blocks, and VirtualAlloc'd blocks over
0x7f000 bytes. The process heap and every program run without the variable are unchanged.

